                <pb facs="1023" n="897" sameAs="frus:frus1925v01/pg897"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.74/516: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Minister in China</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Schurman</hi>)</head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1925-01-28T15:00:00-05:00"><hi rendition="#italic">January 28,
                                1925—3 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>16. Legation’s telegram No. 39 of January 23. The Department would hardly be
                    inclined to reconsider its attitude with respect to the Federal contract save
                    for the fact that a situation has arisen within the Federal Telegraph Company of
                    Delaware which gives rise to the danger that the Radio Corporation may be unable
                    to continue with the project on the original plan. There is some reason to fear,
                    therefore, that in fact the Japanese proposal may prove to be the only available
                    alternative.</p>
                <p>Pending the arrival of Moss and Davis the Department will be glad to have further
                    comment from you in view of these possibilities.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hughes</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
